# Embed First Architecture Directive

The Conis Pilot target product is **Embed** (the partner-facing Experience delivered through Embed.mount).

All analysis, architecture, implementation proposals, refactoring, component design and documentation must treat **Embed as the only production application**.

Client Studio (**Local Runtime**) is **not** the reference implementation. It exists only as an internal developer workspace for debugging, testing and Runtime inspection.

## Delivery surfaces (do not confuse)

| Term | Role |
| --- | --- |
| **Local Runtime** | Developer host of the live Runtime |
| **Embed Demo** | Live Vite partner-style host of the same Runtime |
| **Release Snapshot** | `docs/embed/` from `pnpm embed:publish` |
| **Published Embed** | That snapshot on GitHub Pages / partner URL |

Release Snapshot / Published Embed are **not** Live Runtime. Publish only via `pnpm embed:publish`. See [ADR-019](../../docs/architecture/adr/ADR-019-runtime-vs-release.md).

---

# Two production layers

## 1. Shared Runtime

- `packages/core`
- `packages/runtime`

Runtime owns: Decision Session, Interpretation, Story, Outcome, Terminal, AIContext, Priority processing, Decision Signals.

Runtime is the single source of truth. No business logic may exist outside Runtime.

## 2. Embed Experience

Embed owns: UX, Experience flow, presentation, interaction, surfaces.

Experience flow: Priority → FAQ → Chat → Audit → Report.

Embed consumes Runtime. It never recreates Runtime logic.

---

# Client Studio (Local)

Local is **not** a product. Treat it only as:

- developer workspace
- runtime debugger
- testing environment
- visualization tool

Do **not** use Local as:

- UX reference
- architecture reference
- implementation target
- feature roadmap

---

# Mandatory rules

- Every new feature must be designed for **Embed first**. Never implement features primarily in Local.
- Business logic belongs only to Runtime. Never move Runtime logic into Embed or Local.
- Experience exists only once — Embed is that Experience. Local may visualize it for development only.
- Product-facing components belong to Embed. Developer-facing components belong to Local. Never duplicate product components between them.
- When functionality exists only in Client Studio: do **not** extend it. Classify as: migrate to Embed | move into Runtime | remove | keep as Developer Tool.

---

# Decision rule

Before every proposal, verify:

1. Is this needed in Embed?
2. Does business logic belong in Runtime?
3. Is Local only consuming it?

If any answer is "no", redesign.

---

# Refactoring policy

When Local and Embed differ: **Embed is the reference**.

Objective: eliminate duplicate product implementations — not synchronize two applications.

---

# Long-term target

```text
Shared Runtime
      ↓
Embed Experience
      ↓
Developer Workspace (Local)
```

Local becomes a thin developer shell over the same Runtime and Experience components.

---

# Agent behaviour

- Never propose Local-first implementations.
- Never reference Client Studio as the desired UX.
- Never introduce Local-only product functionality.
- Always optimize for Embed.
- Prefer migration over duplication.
- Prefer Runtime reuse over new abstractions.

When uncertain, ask: **"How would this work in Embed?"** — not "How is this currently implemented in Local?"

Embed is the canonical product.
